{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Know for a certainty that your seed shall be a stranger, etc.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), its explanation is: 'your seed shall be a stranger in a land that is not theirs' for four hundred years, 'and they shall serve them and they shall afflict them'; and it did not specify how long the days of servitude and affliction would be." "<b>And the sage</b> Rabbi Abraham said: 'Know for a certainty that your seed shall be a stranger in a land that is not theirs,' in servitude and affliction, 'until the end of four hundred years from this day.' And according to this He informed him of the end of the redemption, and He informed him how long the days of the exile would be." "<b>Four hundred years.</b> The reason for this number in the Egyptian exile is not known, and not one of the Torah's commentators declares or makes heard a word on this matter." "<b>However,</b> if you examine closely you will find that this number is mentioned in most places in connection with the evil eye, for the evil eye — its number is four hundred, and the letter tav is the last of all the letters, and so the evil eye consumes everything. And because the letter heh was added to Abraham — and it is מדה״ד (the attribute of strict judgment) — therefore the evil eye held sway over him, for it is from the force of the judgment decreed upon his seed to be enslaved four hundred years, corresponding to the number of עי״ן הר״ע (the evil eye), because the eye of the Adversary held sway over him. And this is what it says: 'know for a certainty that your seed shall be a stranger' — that is, your seed born between the two letters heh, which are מדה״ד, and the evil eye held sway over him, for it is from the force of judgment; therefore 'and they shall serve them and they shall afflict them four hundred years.' And so you will find concerning Ephron the Hittite, that he had an evil eye, and עפרו״ן (Ephron) in gematria is עי״ן ר״ע (evil eye), just as it mentioned 'four hundred.' And so you will find concerning Esau, who fed off the attribute of Isaac, which is מדה״ד, that he had an evil eye; and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Genesis 32:17): 'and you shall put a space (רוח) between drove and drove' — why did Jacob say this? In order to sate the eye of that wicked man. And concerning him it is written: 'and four hundred men with him.'" "<b>And I found</b> in Bereshit Rabbah (Daniel 7:8): 'and these eyes were like the eyes of a man in its midst' — this is the kingdom of Aram, which introduces the evil eye into a person's property:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {4 "Source cites 'דניאל ז׳:ח׳' — the verse quoted is Daniel 7:8, not from Bereshit Rabbah; the citation as printed appears to conflate the scriptural reference with a midrashic source. The identity of the 'Bereshit Rabbah' reference here is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:41.592087055Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}